USER   what it should do ("Returns the result of applying `f`", not "Return ..." or
USER   "Should return ..."). For a method/def, start with a third-person verb ("Returns ...",
USER   "Creates ...", "Validates ..."). For a class/trait/object, a noun phrase
USER   ("A `Source` backed by ...") or a verb phrase ("Provides ...") both read well.
USER - NO SELF-REFERENCE: do not begin with "This class/trait/object/method ...". Describe
USER   the thing directly (the style guide prefers "Does XXX" over "This class does XXX").
USER - Add further sentences only when they convey non-obvious behavior, constraints,
USER   side effects, or usage. For a trivial accessor, one sentence is the whole doc.
USER - Do NOT restate the signature ("a method that takes an Int and returns a String").
USER - Match the voice and altitude of nearby Scaladoc in the same file.
USER - HONESTY OVER CONFIDENCE: if the code does not let you determine the intended
USER   meaning, write your best-guess description AND append on its own line, inside
USER   the comment, a marker: `@note NEEDS-HUMAN: <the specific question you could not
USER   resolve>`. A flagged honest guess is far more useful than a confident wrong doc.
USER 
USER TAGS:
USER - @param: purpose of the parameter; constraints (non-negative, non-null), units
USER   (ms, bytes), defaults — only what is true per the code.
USER - @tparam: what the type parameter represents and any bounds.
USER - @return: first decide whether to KEEP or DROP it, using the project's redundant-@return
USER   rule (same as remove-redundant-returns):
USER   * DROP it (delete the whole `@return TODO FILL IN` line) ONLY when BOTH hold:
USER     (a) the comment's description (the prose before the tags) BEGINS with the word
USER         "Returns" — whether that description already existed or you just wrote it, AND
USER     (b) that description already fully states what is returned, so an @return would only
USER         restate it and add no edge case, sentinel, unit, bound, or guarantee.
USER     The very first word of the description must be exactly "Returns". If it begins with any
USER     other word ("Creates", "Computes", "Gets", "A", ...), KEEP the @return.
USER     When you delete the tag, also delete an orphaned blank ` *` line if removing the tag
USER     leaves one with no tags after it. Change no indentation otherwise.
USER   * Otherwise KEEP it and fill it with specifics NOT already in the description — edge
USER     cases ("`None` if absent", "-1 if not found"), units, or a NEW vs mutated collection.
USER   * Be CONSERVATIVE: when in doubt, KEEP and fill. Drop only clear-cut echoes of a
USER     "Returns ..." description.
USER 
USER WORKED EXAMPLE OF THE DROP CASE — read this one twice.
USER Filling in every `@return` is the single most common way this task is got wrong.
USER The rule is a genuine either/or, and the DROP half applies just as often as KEEP.
USER 
USER   Given:
USER       /** TODO FILL IN
USER        *
USER        *  @tparam U TODO FILL IN
USER        *  @param default TODO FILL IN
USER        *  @return TODO FILL IN
USER        */
USER       override def getOrElse[U >: T](default: => U): U = default
USER 
USER   Suppose you write the description "Returns the given `default` argument since
USER   this `Try` is a `Failure`." It begins with the word "Returns" and it already
USER   states the entire return value. So `@return` can only echo it, and the whole
USER   `@return` line MUST be deleted:
USER 
USER   CORRECT:
USER       /** Returns the given `default` argument since this `Try` is a `Failure`.
USER        *
USER        *  @tparam U the type of the returned value, a supertype of `T`
USER        *  @param default the default value to return if this is a `Failure`
USER        */
USER 
USER   WRONG — do not do this:
USER       /** Returns the given `default` argument since this `Try` is a `Failure`.
USER        *
USER        *  @tparam U the type of the returned value, a supertype of `T`
USER        *  @param default the default value to return if this is a `Failure`
USER        *  @return the `default` value          <-- pure echo, must be deleted
USER        */
USER 
USER   CONTRAST, a legitimate KEEP on a neighbouring method:
USER       /** Returns the given `default` argument since this `Try` is a `Failure`.
USER        *
USER        *  @param default the fallback `Try` to return (evaluated lazily)
USER        *  @return the `default` `Try`; any non-fatal exception thrown while
USER        *          evaluating `default` is caught and returned as a `Failure`
USER        */
USER   Here the tag states an exception guarantee the description does not, so it
USER   earns its place. That is the test: does the tag add a fact, or repeat one?
USER 
USER   If removing an `@return` leaves the comment with no tags at all, also remove
USER   the now-orphaned blank ` *` line, so a bare one-line comment stays one line:
USER       /** Returns the value held by this `Success`. */
USER 
USER STYLE (per the Scala style guide):
USER - Prefer wiki-style and markdown over HTML: use `[[scala.Option]]`-style links and
USER   backticks for code references like `pos`; do not introduce HTML tags.
USER - Tag continuations start lowercase and omit a trailing period for single phrases.
USER - Description sentences are full sentences (capitalized, end with a period).
USER - Keep the description text starting on the first line of the comment (after `/**`),
USER   as the existing comments do; do not reflow or re-indent the asterisks.
USER 
USER STRICT SCOPE — this is important:
USER - Replace ONLY the literal `TODO FILL IN` text. Leave every other character of every
